# SRG-APP-000429-WSR-000113  -  CAT I
# The web server must encrypt user identifiers and passwords.
#
# Generated from the benchmark and the assessment determinations. Edit this
# file to change how this requirement is assessed here; it is read at run
# time. Regenerating overwrites it.

id:              SRG-APP-000429-WSR-000113
group:           V-206431
severity:        high
cat:             CAT I
determination:   APPLICABLE
grade:           C
decidable:       machine
confidence:      documented
cci:             CCI-002476
control_rev5:    SC-28

contribution:    Where Splunk Enterprise is itself the user authenticator, it stores
                 passwords for its native authentication only as salted hashes, and the
                 hash algorithm is selectable; running the instance in FIPS mode constrains
                 that and every other cryptographic operation to FIPS Publication 140-2
                 algorithms. Where authentication is delegated, the credentials are not
                 Splunk's to store.

governs:         authentication.conf - How users sign in, and the password rules applied to local accounts.
governs:         splunk-launch.conf - Environment settings read before Splunk starts, including whether it
                 starts in FIPS mode.

check:           authentication.conf [authentication] passwordHashAlgorithm matches ^SHA512-crypt(-[0-9]+)?$
check:           splunk-launch.conf [] SPLUNK_FIPS in 1|true|t|yes (matched case-insensitively)
# --- vendor verification: generated, do not edit by hand
corrected:       splunk-launch.conf [] SPLUNK_FIPS equals 1 -> splunk-launch.conf [] SPLUNK_FIPS in 1|true|t|yes (matched case-insensitively)
vendor:          authentication.conf [authentication] passwordHashAlgorithm https://help.splunk.com/en/splunk-enterprise/administer/admin-manual/10.4/configuration-file-reference/10.4.0-configuration-file-reference/authentication.conf
vendor:          splunk-launch.conf [] SPLUNK_FIPS https://help.splunk.com/en/splunk-enterprise/administer/admin-manual/10.4/configuration-file-reference/10.4.0-configuration-file-reference/splunk-launch.conf
# --- end vendor verification

external:        Where authType is LDAP, SAML, Scripted or ProxySSO, credential storage
                 belongs to the directory service or identity provider and Splunk holds no
                 password to protect - that component's configuration; the SRG's own
                 not-applicable clause governs where Splunk is not the user authenticator
                 and manager
external:        File system ownership and permissions on the password file under the
                 instance's etc directory - operating system
external:        Encryption of the file system holding that file - operating system or
                 storage layer
external:        Protection of credentials held for the instance's own outbound connections
                 in its secret store - assessed separately against the credential storage
                 requirements

citation:        authentication.conf.spec:58-79 (passwordHashAlgorithm =
                 [SHA512-crypt|SHA256-crypt|SHA512-crypt-<num_rounds>|SHA256-crypt-<num_rounds>|MD5-crypt];
                 "This controls how hashed passwords are stored in the
                 $SPLUNK_HOME/etc/passwd file for the default 'Splunk' authType"; MD5-crypt
                 runs 1000 MD5 operations; the SHA algorithms use 5000 rounds and the
                 rounds count must be at least 1000; default SHA512-crypt);
                 authentication.conf.spec:30-33 (authType =
                 [Splunk|LDAP|Scripted|SAML|ProxySSO], default Splunk);
                 splunk-launch.conf.spec:110-116 (SPLUNK_FIPS = [0|1]);
                 authorize.conf.spec:67-72 ([role_<roleName>])
finding:         Stored credentials are not protected to the extent the product allows: the
                 native password hash algorithm is set to MD5-crypt or a SHA-256 variant
                 rather than SHA-512, or FIPS mode is not enabled to constrain the
                 algorithms used.
